On Mar 3, 9:40 pm, "nmi" <iverson.n...@gmail.com> wrote:
> It seems everyone on RGRN loves alchemy of potions. It certainly makes
> the game easier, doesn't it. So why not implement a conduct? Here are
> some ideas:
>
> 1) No dipping of potions into things
> 2) No swimming to dilute potions
> 3) No dipping of objects into potions.
> 4) No polymorph of potions.
>
> I would be in favor of 1+2+3+4 = did not perform alchemy
> or the silly1+3=avoided dipping.
>
> What do you think? Is this yet another stupid ascension trick?
>
> -N

Well, lets see.
First, #4, No polymorph of potions. Well, that is covered in the more
restrictive "Never polymorphed an object". Stupid Ascension Trick
25.1.1 and a currently checked-for conduct.
#3, No dipping of things into potions. Stupid Ascension Trick 7.1.2.
#2, No swimming to dilute. Well, no, this is not covered. But, I'm not
sure it is necessary. It is useful mainly to change potions to water
(with a minor use to make some foo potions stackable with dilute foo
potions prior to blessing/cursing them with a single dip into a holy/
unholy water). OK, you can make water, but is it useful if you are not
going to dip things into it (see above) or quaff it? (SAT 7.1.1 is no
quaffing). I'm not sure this needs a SAT designation.
And #1, No dipping potions to other things. Correct me if I am wrong,
but the only things that potions can be dipped into are fountains or
water, which is functionally the same as #2, swimming to dilute, or
other potions, which is the same as #3.

So far, I don't think I'm going to add to the list of stupid tricks
for diluting potions.

(If you want the current list, it is at http://thinksystem.net/nethack.)

